//! ID tables of the Final Fantasy games NeoFFSE edits.
//!
//! A table holds ids, kinds and names in English and French, plus the game's
//! own caps (`MAX_GIL` and the like). The text lives in
//! `data/pr/<game>/*.toml`, generated by `tools/pr-tables` from the game's
//! master data, and `include_str!` embeds it at build time. No game asset
//! ships here: facts only. Only FF I has data today, and the crate has no
//! `maps` table yet.
//!
//! [`tables_for`] gives the parsed tables of a game, [`GameTables::parse`]
//! parses any set of seven texts, two of them optional, and callers look entries up by [`TableKind`] and id.
//! Names repeat inside a table, so an id is the only key. The role of the
//! crate among the others is in `docs/architecture.md#crate-roles`.
//!
//! The parser checks the file contract, not only the types: ids start at 1
//! and rise, a `kind` string is one of the known kinds, an ability `level` is
//! 0 to 8 and its `content_id` is not 0, `placeholder` appears only as `true`,
//! only on an equipment kind and once per kind, and every cap key starts with
//! `MAX_` and holds no negative value. A row that breaks a value rule gives
//! [`DataError::Rule`]. `growth.toml` holds base HP and vitality per class, the
//! total experience per level and the growth rows of the level-up rule, one
//! array per column and growth group. It has its own rules: status ids rise
//! from 1, `total_exp` starts at 0 for level 1 and rises strictly, `exp_table`
//! has `MAX_LV` rows when `bounds.toml` names that cap, group and job ids rise,
//! every group starts at level 2 and covers every level of `exp_table` in each
//! column, `hp_value1` is 0 or 1, every job names an existing group, and no
//! table is empty. `monsters.toml` and `growth.toml` may be absent: a game
//! without them has no monster table and its growth accessors give `None`.
//!
//! - [`embedded`]: the tables the crate ships, [`sources_for`] and [`tables_for`].
//! - [`entry`]: the rows of a table, [`Entry`], its [`AbilityInfo`] and
//!   [`AbilityKind`] for an ability row, and the [`Language`] of a name.
//! - [`error`]: [`DataError`], why a set of texts failed to parse.
//! - [`tables`]: [`GameTables`], the tables and caps of one game, and [`Sources`], the seven texts, two of them optional, that build them.
//!
//! ```
//! use neoffse_core::{Game, TableKind};
//! use neoffse_data::{Language, tables_for};
//!
//! let tables = tables_for(Game::Ff1Pr).ok_or("no FF I tables")?;
//! let potion = tables.entry(TableKind::Item, 2).ok_or("no item 2")?;
//! assert_eq!(potion.name(Language::En), "Potion");
//! assert_eq!(tables.bound("MAX_GIL"), Some(999_999));
//! assert_eq!(tables.base_hp(1), Some(35));
//! assert_eq!(tables.base_vitality(1), Some(15));
//! assert_eq!(tables.exp_to_reach(2), Some(14));
//! # Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>(())
//! ```
//!
//! [`TableKind`]: neoffse_core::TableKind

